  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA slow moving upper level low will remain to our west through\nSunday, before slowly moving eastward Sunday night into Monday.\nExpect waves of showers/thunderstorms to move through the CWA from\nnow through Sunday afternoon. There is the potential that a few\nstorms could become severe on Sunday, thus much of the CWA is in a\nmarginal risk for severe storms. As the upper level low moves\neastward Sunday night into Monday, the showers will change more\ninto a stratiform rain, with the heaviest rainfall expected over\nNorth Central MT. There is also a marginal risk for excessive\nrainfall that could cause flash flooding Sunday night into Mon\nover North Central MT.\n\nDrier and warmer conditions develop mid week, with afternoon\ntemperatures close to 10 degrees above normal by Thursday. By next\nFriday, another upper level trof will start to move southward into\nthe Pacific Northwest, resulting in a moist southwesterly flow\nover Montana. Thus the chances for precipitation will start to\nincrease once again next Fri/Sat.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nThe main concern on Sunday for any severe storms will be strong\nwind gusts. This system on Sunday will not be as strong as the\nweather system that went through the CWA on Wednesday, never the\nless, an isolated severe storm is possible. Additionally, some\nstorms could also produce heavy rainfall. By Monday night, the\nnorthern portions of North Central MT could have received between\n1 and 3 inches of rainfall, with isolated amounts near 4 inches\npossible. Overall, flooding is not expected, but in areas that\nexceed 3 inches, the probability of minor flooding on small\nstreams and creaks starts to increase. Brusda",
